KEYWORDS: enhancement of Raman scattering, hollow-core anti-resonant optical fiber, strong light−matter interaction, single exosome detection, cancer diagnosis
Microstructured optical fibers (MOFs), especially the most recent innovations of hollow-core anti-resonant optical fibers (HcARFs), provide solutions for breaking through the bottlenecks in areas of high-power transmission and high-efficiency optical waveguides. The featured light-guiding properties of ARFs determine their unique advantages such as broadband transmission, strong light−matter interaction, low delay, low nonlinearity, low transmission loss, and low dispersion, rendering them an ideal medium for communication, especially in the big data era.
Other than transporting light waves, HcARFs can synergistically combine microfluidics and optics in a single fiber with an unprecedented light path length not readily achievable by planar optofluidic configurations. The combination of featured parameters allows ultrafast signal propagation through the fiber without significant distortions, opening a door to a vast range of biochemical applications. A recently reported low-nonlinearity hollow-core ARF can transmit a single pair of orthogonal polarization modes with cross-coupling on a scale of \(10^{-10}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\), providing a leap in the performance of photonics-enabled sensors and instruments. As a fluid channel, HcARFs enable the nL to \(\mu\mathrm{L}\) level sample to be tested in a gaseous or liquid form in the core, dispensing with a sample cell. Therefore, HcARFs have emerged as a powerful tool in the field of biochemical, gas, strain, and temperature sensing, via taking advantages of the strong light−matter interaction. Light−gas−acoustic interaction and light−gas−thermal interaction in gas-filled HcARFs resulted in the phase modulation of a probe beam propagating in the fiber, enabling spectroscopic characterization of gas species and concentration
of \(10^{-10}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\) , providing a leap in the performance of photonicsenabled sensors and instruments.3 As a fluid channel, HcARFs enable the nL to \(\mu\mathrm{L}\) level sample to be tested in a gaseous or liquid form in the core, dispensing with a sample cell. Therefore, HcARFs have emerged as a powerful tool in the field of biochemical, gas, strain, and temperature sensing,4−9 via taking advantages of the strong light−matter interaction. Light−gas−acoustic interaction and light−gas−thermal interaction in gas-filled HcARFs resulted in the phase modulation of a probe beam propagating in the fiber, enabling spectroscopic characterization of gas species and concentration
as well as the fiber microstructure.\(^{7-9}\) Orders-of-magnitude enhancement of the fluorescent signal by HcARF enabled the ultrasensitive detection of environmental pollutants.\(^{6,10}\)
In view of enhancing Raman scattering using optical fibers, most reported studies were focused on using hollow core photonic crystal fibers (HcPCFs). Previously, great successes have been achieved in enhancing Raman scattering via free-space propagation in order to achieve highly sensitive detection; for instance, in surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS),\(^{11}\) tip-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (TERS),\(^{12}\) and cavity-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (CERS),\(^{13}\) the most striking enhancement factor (EF) by several orders of magnitude has been reported. Compared with free-space propagation, there are advantages of using optical fibers to enhance the Raman signal. For example, optical fibers are gifted with a striking cumulative effect due to the extended length. For most of the cases, the volume of light−sample interaction of free-space propagation, which is proportional to the Raman signal intensity, is extremely small. Tiny impurities would severely interfere with the assays, hindering the applications of the existing mechanisms. Considering the complexity of biological and clinical samples, the low content of the target, as well as the necessity of point-of-care monitoring, additional technologies favoring clinical applications are in urgent demand. In this regard, a marriage of optical fiber approach with SERS/TERS/CERS may provide a solution.
Using HcPCFs, highly sensitive detection of the cancer marker epidermal growth factor receptors (EGFRs), heparin, leukemia cells, and so forth has been reported.\(^{14-19}\) However, HcPCFs suffer from numerous drawbacks that limit their use for SERS-based biosensing and other opto-fluidic applications,\(^{20}\) which include the following: (a) the transmission window is rather narrow, (b) the properties could be deteriorated by light coupling to modes confined in the ring of glass surrounding the hollow core,\(^{21}\) and (c) the evanescent field and the cladding mode of HcPCF often generate a strong background Raman signal of the silica material, interfering with the measurements. To block the background signal, an additional device, such as a spatial filtering system with a small pinhole, needs to be adopted.\(^{15}\) These weaknesses severely hamper the applications of the Raman-based HcPCF sensor, especially for the real-time monitoring of clinical samples. Other types of optical fibers, such as a metal-lined hollow core fiber and a suspended core optical fiber, have also been investigated for sensing applications; however, the transmission loss is rather high and, sometimes, is not appropriate for specific and label-free detection.\(^{22-24}\)
The advantages of state-of-the-art HcARFs have attracted increasing attention in the sensing area. It is in this context that we investigated the Raman enhancing effects of HcARFs and subsequently developed a novel HcARF-based SERS sensing platform for label-free and ultrasensitive detection of cancer exosomes in an extremely low sample volume. As a proof of concept, cancer-derived exosomes were analyzed quantitatively. Highly specific single exosome probing was achieved; moreover, surface protein profiling and multiplexed exosome identification were explored to allow the assignment of cancer subtypes.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Fabrication and Characterization of HcARFs. Silica tubes were pre-drawn into capillaries and then stacked in the silica outer sleeve to
form an optical fiber preform. The fiber preform was placed in the high-temperature drawing tower and was drawn to form the designated microstructure.
The cross-sectional morphology of the as-fabricated optical fiber was analyzed using a scanning electronic microscope (SU9000 STEM/SEM, Hitachi High-Technologies Corporation of Japan).
The transmission band was measured using a supercontinuum light source. Briefly, the laser of the supercontinuum light source was coupled into the \(10~\mathrm{cm}\) HcARF (or LcARF). The spot at the output end was recorded with a charge-coupled device, and the transmission spectrum was recorded by a spectrometer.
Numerical Simulation. The light field distribution of HcARF and LcARF and the transmission losses were simulated using COMSOL Multiphysics. Only confinement loss was analyzed. The effective refractive indices of HcARF and LcARF at different wavelengths of optical transmission were utilized. According to the confinement loss formula, the loss value in the \(0.4{-}1.5~\mu\mathrm{m}\) band was simulated.
Raman Scattering Signal Measurement. For the planar substrate experiment, \(10~\mu\mathrm{L}\) Ag-4-MBN solutions at different 4-MBN concentrations were dropped on glass slides followed by immediate acquisition of the Raman signal as described below. For optical fiber and capillary experiments, the same length \(\left(10\;\mathrm{cm}\right)\) of the capillary \(\left(50\ \mu\mathrm{m}\right)\) diameter, TSU050375, Polymicro Technologies) and HcARF fiber was filled with different concentrations of Ag-4-MBN solution via an autosampler. Raman spectra were acquired using a confocal Raman spectrometer (Renishaw inVia) with a \(50\times\) objective. A \(785~\mathrm{nm}\) laser was used to excite the samples. Data acquisition conditions were kept same for all the samples (excitation power \(0.6\;\mathrm{mW}\), acquisition time \(10~\mathrm{s}\)). The beam size of the excitation light was about \(1~\mu\mathrm{m}\). The signal obtained was from the total excitation zone.
The Raman EF was calculated using eq. \(1^{24}\)
\[
\mathrm{EF}=\frac{\frac{I_{\mathrm{F}}}{C_{\mathrm{N}}}}{\frac{I_{\mathrm{N}}}{C_{\mathrm{N}}}}
\]
where \(I_{\mathrm{F}}\) represents the intensity of the Raman signal in LcARF and \(I_{\mathrm{N}}\) represents the intensity of the Raman signal on the planar substrate. \(C_{\mathrm{F}}\) and \(C_{\mathrm{N}}\) are the concentrations of the 4-MBN molecule.
Fiber Surface Functionalization. A mixture of (3-aminopropyl)-triethoxysilane (APTES), absolute ethanol, and ultrapure water at a concentration of 2.5, 96.5, and \(1\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) was injected into the fiber channel using a precision injector (Bading Leifu Fluid Technology Co., Ltd., China). After standing at room temperature for \(6~\mathrm{h}\), the fiber was washed with water. The carboxylated AptCD63 was first activated by an ethyl (dimethylamino propyl) carbodiimide/N-hydroxy succinimide (EDC/NHS) solution (EDC and NHS were mixed in a 1:1 ratio, then added to a \(40~\mu\mathrm{L}\) \(\mathrm{pH} = 5.85\) [2-(N-morpholino)ethanesulfonic acid] (MES) buffer containing AptCD63) before being filled into the fiber channel. After overnight incubation at room temperature, bovine serum albumin \((1~\mathrm{mg/mL})\) was filled into the fiber channel to shield the electrostatically active sites on the inner wall of the fiber. AptCD63-Cy5 was immobilized on the fiber wall using a similar protocol. The above-mentioned functionalized fiber was washed with water and then placed in the optical path shown in Figure S3 for fluorescence measurement.
Construction of SERS Probes. To prepare SERS probes for HER2, \(4~\mu\mathrm{L}\) \(5~\mu\mathrm{M}\) AptH2 was added to \(100~\mu\mathrm{L}\) \(0.03~\mathrm{nM}\) AgNPs solution and incubated at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). Then, \(8~\mu\mathrm{L}\) \(0.7~\mathrm{mM}\) 4-MBN was added and incubated at room temperature for \(3~\mathrm{h}\). The resultant SERS probes were purified by centrifugation (Thermo Scientific) at \(8000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~\mathrm{min}\), followed by the removal of the supernatant. The probes were then dispersed in phosphate-buffered saline. \(5~\mu\mathrm{L}\) of SERS probes was dried on a silicon wafer for Raman spectrum measurement with a confocal Raman spectrometer. The zeta potential of SERS probes was measured using a Mastersizer 3000 (Malvern Panalytical).
Exosome Detection Using the Proposed Sensor. The SkBr3 exosomes were filled into the functionalized ARF using a precision
liquid filling instrument at a speed of \(1\,\mu\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}\) and then incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\). Then, the SERS probe for HER2 was streamed in the fiber and incubated overnight at room temperature to allow the HER2 probe to specifically bind to SKBR3 exosomes. After being thoroughly washed with ultrapure water, the fiber was placed on the operating platform for Raman spectra acquisition. A confocal Raman spectrometer (Renishaw inVia) with a \(50\times\) objective was used to detect SERS spectra. The SERS spectra were acquired at \(785\,\mathrm{nm}\) excitation (the power was \(0.6\,\mathrm{mW}\)); the integration time was \(10\,\mathrm{s}\).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Fabrication and Characterization of HcARFs. The light guiding mechanism of the anti-resonant fiber (ARF) follows the principle of the anti-resonant reflecting optical waveguide, as shown in eq 2, where \(n\) is the refractive index of the fiber material, i.e., silica \((n=1.45)\), \(n_{1}\) is the refractive index of the core, \(t\) is the wall thickness of the seven cladding holes, and \(m=0,1,2....\mathrm{HcARFs}\) allow the matching of low refractive index (RI) fluids, resulting in a strong light-sample overlap in the core.
\[
\lambda_{\mathrm{res}}=\frac{2t}{m}\sqrt{n^2-n_1^2}
\]
The resonance condition is usually determined by the thickness of the cladding tube wall and the silica refractive index. The core size and cladding tube size of HcARF were premeditated according to the required transmission wavelength and single-mode transmission characteristics. Then, the HcARF fiber was fabricated using the stack-and-draw method. Briefly, the capillary tubes were stacked inside to form a preform and drawn uniformly at high temperatures. The cross section of as-fabricated HcARFs is shown in Figure 1a. The outer diameter was \(202\,\mu\mathrm{m}\), the air core diameter \(D\) was \(30\,\mu\mathrm{m}\), the cladding tube diameter was \(14\,\mu\mathrm{m}\), and the wall thickness \(t\) of the cladding tube was \(0.4\,\mu\mathrm{m}\).
cross section of as-fabricated HcARFs is shown in Figure 1a. The outer diameter was \(202\ \mu\mathrm{m},\) the air core diameter \(D\) was \(30\ \mu\mathrm{m},\) the cladding tube diameter was \(14\ \mu\mathrm{m},\) and the wall thickness \(t\) of the cladding tube was \(0.4\ \mu\mathrm{m}\) .
The transmission spectrum is shown in Figure 1b (purple line). The anti-resonant transmission windows of a \(10\,\mathrm{cm}\) HcARF, measured using a supercontinuum light source \((400-1200\,\mathrm{nm})\), were \(470-750\) and \(>830\,\mathrm{nm}\) \((>1000\,\mathrm{nm}\) was not measured). Since the fiber was attempted for sensing aqueous samples, the transmission spectrum of a liquid-core ARF (LcARF) was measured using the same supercontinuum light source, as shown in Figure 1b (orange line). Mainly single-mode guidance was observed for both HcARF and LcARF (Figure 1b inset). Excellent light transmission in the range of \(470-1000\,\mathrm{nm}\) was observed for LcARF, which is in contrast to HcARF. The better light transmission in the range of \(750-830\,\mathrm{nm}\) of LcARF can be explained using eq 2, which evidences that the transmission window of ARF is related to the refractive index contrast between the cladding glass and the core material. The resonant wavelength \(\lambda_{\mathrm{res}}\) decreases when the core material is changed from air \((n=1)\) to water \((n=1.33)\), which is spectrally manifested as a blue shift of the resonant band. As a result, the resonant band (non-light conductive band) for HcARF at \(750-830\,\mathrm{nm}\) converted to light conductive band for LcARF, which is a benefit for using the \(785\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser to excite the Raman signal. Please note that, due to the wavelength limitation of the spectrometer, we were unable to observe the blue shift of the transmission band in the short wavelength region (around \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\)).
A striking feature of HcARFs is the strict light confinement in the fiber core due to the unusual light transmission mechanisms. In addition, the specially designed microstructure also enables light to be propagated at very low transmission loss, in clear contrast to free space transmission. The overall effects ensure strong light-matter interaction in the core region.
To confirm this, the light electric field distribution of the fundamental guiding mode of HcARF and LcARF was simulated using the finite element analysis method, as shown in Figure 1c and Figure 1d. At 785 nm, the light field distribution of LcARF (Figure 1d) showed the maximum intensity at the core center, ensuring strong light-matter interaction within the liquid core. These results provide strong evidence that the liquid core is a robust waveguide, although the refractive index of the filled liquid (n = 1.33) is much higher than that of air (n = 1.0).
The simulated transmission losses of HcARF and LcARF are shown in Figure 1e (here we only simulated the confinement loss). A resonant peak with the maximum wavelength of \(\sim 800\) nm for HcARF was observed (purple line), which is consistent with the transmission band (Figure 1b purple line). For LcARF, the resonant band was present at around 430 nm. This is consistent with the measured transmission spectrum in Figure 1b (orange line). Clearly, this property of LcARF favors Raman signal acquisition using 785 nm excitation wavelength.
ARF Enhances Raman Scattering. To investigate the fiber enhanced Raman scattering, the HcARF was filled with a mixture of AgNPs and 4-mercaptobenzonitrile (4-MBN) solution and excited with a 785 nm laser (Figure S1). Figure 2a−c shows the Raman spectra of 4-MBN from a planar substrate (glass slide), a capillary tube, and an HcARF, respectively. A prominent peak of Raman shift at \(\sim 2223 \, \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was observed owing to the stretch of the \(\mathrm{C} \equiv \mathrm{N}\) triple bond, while the other two prominent peaks of Raman shift at 1585 and 1075 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were assigned to the symmetric in-plane aromatic ring \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) deformation and the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}\) deformation, respectively. Analyzing the data in Figure 2a−c, great Raman signal enhancement was obtained. The Raman enhancing factors (EF) were over three orders of magnitude, which were \(2.43 \times 10^{3}\) and \(4.89 \times 10^{3}\), when compared with LcARF with a capillary tube and LcARF with a planar substrate, respectively, which is the best record for optical fiber enhanced Raman scattering.
The remarkable enhancement of Raman scattering by ARF can be explicated by the joint effects of the strong light-confining ability of ARF and the cumulative effect of the fiber. The former enables maximum light-matter overlap within the liquid core, thus effectively amplifying the Raman scattering signal. Consistent with this, as a negative control, the capillary tube (with the same length and a similar diameter), which was unable to confine light within the liquid core due to the absence of the microstructured geometry, failed to efficiently enhance the Raman signal. The cumulative effect arose from the fact that the ARF fiber elongated the laser beam waist owing to the light confinement effect, therefore allowing the laser beam waist to interact with the extended sample length (Figure 2d) compared to that of the planar substrate where the spot was limited by the scattering volume at the spot's focal point. The Raman scattering signal intensity \((P)\) can be expressed by eq 3.
\[
P\propto\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sigma_{i}I_{i}=M\sigma I_{0}=m\sigma V I_{0}
\]
where \(\sigma\) is the Raman scattering cross section of a molecule, \(I_{0}\) is the average light intensity of the pumping laser at the location, \(V\) is the excited sample volume, and \(m\) is the molecular density. Clearly, increasing the light intensity is an effective way for enhancing the Raman signal, as in the cases of SERS, TERS, and CERS, in which enhancements originate from the localized field enhancement based on the near-field effect of the metal nanostructure or circulated light in the cavity. In the case of the fiber enhanced Raman signal, increasing the excitable sample volume plays a major role in signal enhancement, which may be impractical for free-space propagation, which can be easily realized using ARF. More importantly, not only the ARF fiber provides a means to increase the sample volume, but also the fiber is gifted with the light-converging ability due to the strict light confinement via the special micro-geometry, as shown in Figure 2d, ensuring strong light-matter interaction and thus enabling efficient
enhancement of the Raman signal, which is a striking advantage compared with free-space propagation.
The cumulative effect could be demonstrated by the signal enhancement brought by the increase of fiber length. We explored the effect of fiber length on Raman intensities by back-cutting the fiber, and the results are shown in Figure 2e,f. Raman signal was positively correlated with the fiber length, providing a way to further intensify the signal. Considering the attenuation caused by sample absorption and particle scattering, etc., a maximum intensity would be reached at a certain fiber length. Due to the limitation of the setup (the maximum working distance was \(10\ \mathrm{cm}\)), we were unable to further investigate the effect of fiber length.
ARF Sensor for Exosome Detection. Working Principle of the Sensor. Exosomes are a subclass of extracellular vesicles (EVs) with a diameter of \(40{-}160\ \mathrm{nm}\) generated in a cellular "endocytosis-fusion-exclusion" manner and play a crucial role in cell communication and tissue microenvironment regulation. Exosomes can trace back to the originating cells, reflecting disease progression and prognosis, providing a way to monitor pathological changes of cells or tissues. Therefore, exosome analysis holds great potential for diagnosis, prognosis, and therapy.
Having demonstrated the robust Raman enhancement of HcARF, we then constructed a sensor for exosome identification and quantification. The sensor was designed based on a sandwiched structure via the integration of SERS and ARF enhanced Raman scattering (AERS) as shown in Figure 3.
The major advantage of the sandwich sensor is that high specificity and sensitivity can be achieved without pretreatment of the sample, which can be time consuming and error prone. To construct the sensor, the inner wall of the HcARF fiber is chemically coupled with a CD63 aptamer (AptCD63) to form a capture substrate (Figure 3b); meanwhile, a SERS probe that can specifically recognize cancer exosomes is fabricated, as
shown in Figure 3c. Then, cancer-related exosomes (Figure 3d) are streamed in the fiber microchannel and are thus captured on the inner surface of the fiber. When SERS probes are passed through the fiber channel (Figure 3e), they bind to the cancer-specific exosomes to form a sandwich structure of "fiber capturing substrate-exosomes-SERS probes" as shown in Figure 3f. By detecting the light intensity back-scattering of the fiber after excitation with a \(785~\mathrm{nm}\) laser, the Raman signal can be detected (Figure 3g). This is the first study using ARF enhanced Raman scattering for label-free cancer exosome detection. The proposed sensor would enable (a) high sensitivity through the joint effect of SERS and AERS; (b) high specificity and thus label-free Raman detection; (c) direct sample application without pretreatment; And (d) nano liter level sample consumption.
To construct the sensor, a capture DNA was first immobilized on the fiber wall. The fiber capturing substrate was fabricated based on the recognition ability of the CD63 aptamer (CD63 is a class of surface proteins present in all types of exosomes), which enables the capturing of all types of exosomes in the sample including normal cell exosomes and cancer cell exosomes. The CD63 aptamer (AptCD63, Table S1 for sequence) was chemically coupled to the cladding tube walls of HcARF; please see Figure S2 for the chemistry. The quantity of the capturing aptamer modified on tube walls could determine the quantity of trapped exosomes in the sample. We used a fluorescently labeled CD63 aptamer (AptCD63-Cy5, Table S1) to optimize the capturing aptamer quantity via measuring the fluorescence intensity in the fiber after chemical coupling (the optical setup for measuring the fluorescence in the fiber is shown in Figure S3). The fluorescence intensity increased as the AptCD63-Cy5 concentration elevated and reached the maximum at a concentration of \(20\,\mu\mathrm{M}\) (Figures 4a and S4). Therefore, \(20\,\mu\mathrm{M}\) of AptCD63 was used to construct the sensor for all of the following experiments.
Subsequently, a SERS probe was constructed using Ag nanoparticles (AgNPs). A single strand DNA aptamer against HER2 (H2) protein (AptH2, Table S2 for sequence) and a Raman reporter molecule (4-MBN) were covalently bonded to AgNPs to form the SERS probe (Figure 3c). HER2 is a cancer marker, highly over-expressed in SkBr3 breast cancer cells and exosomes. TEM images of AgNPs-AptH2 showed a thin layer on the surface of AgNPs (Figure 4b,c); the final diameter of the SERS probe was about \(88\,\mathrm{nm}\). The zeta potentials of AgNPs and AgNPs-AptH2 (Figure 4d) were \(-10.08667\) and \(-33.16667\,\mathrm{mV}\), further confirming successful linkage. The UV−vis extinction spectrum of AgNPs-AptH2 showed a slight red shift compared with AgNPs (Figure 4e). The optimized concentrations of AptH2, 4-MBN, and AgNPs were 0.179, 50 \(\mu\mathrm{M}\), and \(0.027\,\mathrm{nM}\), respectively, corresponding to a ratio of \(6.63\times10^{3}{:}1.85\times10^{6}\): 1; see Figures 4f and S5 for details.
To ensure the SERS probe performance, we isolated breast cancer cell SkBr3 derived exosomes and investigated the binding between SERS probes and exosomes. The particle size distribution and morphology of the isolated exosomes are shown in Figures \(4\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}\) and S6. The exosomes showed a typical vesicle structure with an average dimension of \(120\,\mathrm{nm}\) (91% exosomes are in the range of \(110{-}170\ \mathrm{nm}\)). The synthesized SERS probe was mixed with isolated SkBr3 exosomes followed by TEM analysis. The morphology of the probe−exosome complex is shown in Figure 4i. Clearly, the SERS probe bound to the SkBr3 exosome efficiently, in most cases, in a 1:1 ratio. Cancer Exosome Sensing. As a proof of concept, we conducted exosome sensing using the platform. The exosome suspension was reconstituted into various concentrations and subsequently streamed in a \(10\,\mathrm{cm}\) ARF sensor followed by the introduction of the SERS probe. After the redundant probes were washed out, the fiber sensor was excited with a \(785\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser. The Raman signal intensified as the exosome concentration increased as shown in Figure 5a. The intensities (after the subtraction of the background) of the Raman mode at wavenumbers of 1075 and \(1585\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were plotted as a function of exosome concentration (Figure 5b). Clearly, the Raman signals of the two modes increased as the exosome numbers increased in a dose-dependent manner. The peak at the wavenumber of \(1075\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was used to evaluate the quantity of exosomes. In the exosome concentration range of \(8{-}1600\) particles \(/ \mu\mathrm{L}\), the Raman signal showed a good linearity with the exosome concentration (Figure 5b inset). The simulated limit of detection (LoD) was 2.2 particles \(/ \mu\mathrm{L}\) \((\mathrm{LoD}=3\delta/\mathrm{S})\), which means that single exosome detection can be achieved since only about \(253\,\mathrm{mL}\) sample is required for a \(10\,\mathrm{cm}\) HcARF fiber. Figure 5c displays the high-resolution SEM images of the fiber wall prior to (i) and post exosome capture (ii) and post SERS probe binding (iii).
complex is shown in Figure 4i. Clearly, the SERS probe bound to the SkBr3 exosome efficiently, in most cases, in a 1:1 ratio.
Cancer Exosome Sensing. As a proof of concept, we conducted exosome sensing using the platform. The exosome suspension was reconstituted into various concentrations and subsequently streamed in a \(10\,\mathrm{cm}\) ARF sensor followed by the introduction of the SERS probe. After the redundant probes were washed out, the fiber sensor was excited with a \(785\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser. The Raman signal intensified as the exosome concentration increased as shown in Figure 5a. The intensities (after the subtraction of the background) of the Raman mode at wavenumbers of 1075 and \(1585\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were plotted as a function of exosome concentration (Figure 5b). Clearly, the Raman signals of the two modes increased as the exosome numbers increased in a dose-dependent manner. The peak at the wavenumber of \(1075\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was used to evaluate the quantity of exosomes. In the exosome concentration range of \(8{-}1600\) particles \(/ \mu\mathrm{L}\), the Raman signal showed a good linearity with the exosome concentration (Figure 5b inset). The simulated limit of detection (LoD) was 2.2 particles \(/ \mu\mathrm{L}\) \((\mathrm{LoD}=3\delta/\mathrm{S})\), which means that single exosome detection can be achieved since only about \(253\,\mathrm{mL}\) sample is required for a \(10\,\mathrm{cm}\) HcARF fiber. Figure 5c displays the high-resolution SEM images of the fiber wall prior to (i) and post exosome capture (ii) and post SERS probe binding (iii).
To validate the results, we performed a number of control experiments, as shown in Figure 5d. No Raman signal was detected in the absence of the CD63 aptamer (dark red line) or exosomes (dark cyan line), which ruled out the possibility of false positive results. When the AptH2 aptamer on the SERS probes was replaced with a random DNA sequence (dark yellow line; Table S1 for sequence), no detectable Raman signal was obtained either, indicating the high specificity of the system toward cancer marker HER2 overexpressed exosomes. The sensor was fairly robust. Electric field \((15\;\mathrm{V/m})\) and magnetic field \((10\;\mu\mathrm{T})\) could not interfere with the Raman signal (Figure S7), suggesting a benefit of optical fiber sensing (electric and magnetic fields would be supposed to interfere with some assays, for instance, electrochemical sensors).
The selectivity of the sensor was investigated using a panel of cancer cell (breast cancer cell lines of SkBr3, MDA-MB-231, and T47D and pancreatic cancer cell line Panc-1) derived exosomes. Non-cancerous cell line NIH3T3 derived exosomes were also tested for comparison. As shown in Figure 5d, the Raman intensity of SkBr3 exosomes was significantly stronger than that of other cancer cell derived exosomes, while no detectable Raman signal was obtained for NIH3T3 derived exosomes. The differences are statistically significant (Figure 5e). Therefore, the proposed sensor could selectively distinguish exosomes secreted by H2 overexpressing cancer cells, holding great potential for clinical diagnosis.
Exosome Surface Protein Profiling. Exosomes are highly heterogeneous in molecular composition,32−35 and their surface proteins bear characteristics of their tissues of origin,36,37 rendering specific subclasses of these vesicles promising to demonstrate the pathology affecting specific tissues.38−40 The distinct characteristics of exosomal surface markers in different diseases also manifest them as a promising basis for disease classification. Therefore, we investigated the surface protein profiling of various types of exosomes derived from cancer cells.
To this end, we fabricated a panel of SERS probes, as shown in Table S2. Each probe was designed to recognize a specific surface marker of cancer cell (ER, MUC1, EGFR, and EpCAM) and was attached with a distinct Raman molecule (4-Mpy, 2NAT, 4-NBT, and MGITC, respectively). We measured the protein expression on the surface of six types of cellular exosomes, including SkBr3, NIH3T3, Panc 01, MDA-MB-231, MCF7, and T47D.
Different types of exosomes isolated from different cell culturing media were reconstituted to the same concentration of 400 particles/ \(\mu\mathrm{L}\) and streamed in the fiber channel, followed by the introduction of the SERS probe and acquisition of the Raman signal. The obtained data are shown in Figure 6. With the ER-specific SERS probe, the fiber sensor gave rise to the highest response to exosomes of MCF7 \((1004\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) wavenumber) (Figure 6a). Other exosomes only generated low level Raman signal; the difference is statistically significant (Figure
S8a). With the MUC1-specific SERS probe (Figures 6b and S8b), the highest response was observed for exosomes of MCF7 and MDA-MB-231 cells (1378 cm\(^{-1}\) wavenumber), while exosomes of T47D resulted in moderate Raman response and exosomes of other three cell lines resulted in the lowest Raman signal. With the EGFR-specific SERS probe, the highest response was observed for exosomes of the MDA-MB-231 cell (wavenumber 1332 cm\(^{-1}\)) (Figures 6c and S8c), while moderate responses were observed for exosomes of SkBr3 and Panc-1 and the lowest responses were observed for other three type of exosomes. With the EpCAM-specific SERS probe, the highest response was obtained from exosomes of the SkBr3 cell (wavenumber 1616 cm\(^{-1}\)); other exosomes hardly generated any Raman response (Figures 6d and S8d).
The data are plotted in a heatmap as shown in Figure 6ei. An analysis of the protein expression profile of different types of exosomes reveals that the five cancer markers exist in all five cancer cell line derived exosomes; expression levels are distinctly different though. These data verify the capability of the fiber sensor to differentiate the subtle variation of protein levels in exosomes from different cell types. The data indicate that the panel of exosomal protein information can be used to identify the source cells of exosomes; please refer to Figure 6e(ii). For example, because EpCAM and HER2 were shown
to be cancer biomarkers, their exosomal levels can serve as the first criterion to distinguish HER2(+) cancer cells from normal and other cancerous cells (Figure 6e); similarly, ER and MUC1 can be used to distinguish the MCF7 type of cancer from others. Consequently, the SERS-based ARF sensor detection of a panel of biomarkers is able to distinguish exosomes secreted from five different cells.
Finally, the feasibility of the multiplexed detection of different types of exosomes were also investigated by taking advantages of the platform. The mixture of three types of exosomes (SkBr3, MCF7, and MDA-MB-231) in a 1:1:1 ratio was filled in the fiber channel after AptCD63 was fixed on the fiber wall. Then, a mixture of three SERS probes for HER2, ER, and MUC1 was streamed in the channel. Subsequently, the Raman signal was acquired with the excitation of a 785 nm laser, as shown in Figure 6f. The intensities of the dominant bands highlighted in Figure 6f, i.e., 1004 cm\(^{-1}\) for ER, 1378 cm\(^{-1}\) for MUC1, and 2223 cm\(^{-1}\) for HER2 (please note that 1075 cm\(^{-1}\) was used for the specific analysis of the HER2-positive exosome in the aforementioned single probe assay; for the multiplexed assay, since the MUC1 probe shows a Raman mode at 1068 cm\(^{-1}\), very close to 1075 cm\(^{-1}\), to avoid ambiguity, the 2223 cm\(^{-1}\) mode was chosen to distinguish HER2 from others), can simultaneously reflect the presence of
the corresponding exosomes of MCF7, MDA-MB-231, and SkBr3. The success of the simultaneous multiple detection of exosomes is significant for the application of this method in clinical diagnostics.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have investigated the Raman enhancing effect of HcARFs; several orders of magnitude enhancement was achieved owing to the specific light-confinement property and cumulative effect of the ARF fiber. Stemming from here, a sandwich structured fiber sensor was developed. Sensitive detection of breast cancer SkBr3 secreted exosomes down to a single exosome level was realized. Analyzing the Raman signals in response to a panel of cancer exosomes allow us to distinguish different types of cancer. It is well known that exosomes carry proteins and nucleic acids related to the types and the corresponding metabolic status of the parent cells. Such exosomal information can be considered as a fingerprint of its parent cells and be potentially used for the identification of cancer cells. The proposed platform provides a way to further enhance the Raman signal on the top of SERS, holding great potential for point-of-care testing.
The SERS-ARF platform could also be used for therapeutic purposes for the delivery to specific parts of the body accurately and quickly and monitoring drug treatment responses in real time. The superiority SERS tags over the fluorescent counterparts in terms of multiplexing capability, low toxicity, and clearance efficiency is well documented in in vivo studies, and the combination of the SERS tag with state-of-the-art HcARF would open up a new door for supersensitive biomarker detection. Hereafter, such a platform could be exploited in the future to realize a clinically viable, real-time sensing tool.
Since the discovery of SERS, large efforts have been devoted to the improvement of EF. Our findings provide an efficient way to additionally enhance the Raman signal on the top of SERS. Taken together, the ongoing tremendous development in HcARF combined with the smart design of SERS tags could ultimately lead to a rapid, sensitive diagnostic tool for patients.
